# An ownership marker moves, lends or freezes a variable or a field. A literal
# names no storage, so a marker on one would do nothing while saying it does.
# `5.take` and `"abc".hold` were accepted and meant their value; `"abc".borrow`
# took the address of a const static, `true.take` read an arm named `take`, and
# `5.u8.take` spelled the marker as a literal suffix. `.drop` ends a variable's
# lifetime, and a literal is not one.
main: function is {
    a: 5.take
    b: 5.borrow
    c: 2.5.hold
    d: "abc".take
    e: "abc".borrow
    f: "abc".hold
    g: true.take
    h: 5.u8.take
    5.drop
    print "\{a} \{b} \{c} \{d} \{e} \{f} \{g} \{h}"
}
